<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Neural Plast</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">NP</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Neural Plasticity</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">2090-5904</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1687-5443</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">16444901</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2565471</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1155/NP.2005.299</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Genetic Mouse Models of Alzheimer's Disease</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Mineur</surname><given-names>Yann S.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"><sup>1</sup></xref><xref ref-type="corresp" rid="cor1">*</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>McLoughlin</surname><given-names>Declan</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I2"><sup>2</sup></xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Crusio</surname><given-names>Wim E.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I3"><sup>3</sup></xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Sluyter</surname><given-names>Frans</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I4"><sup>4</sup></xref></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="I1"><sup>1</sup>Yale School of Medicine, Department of Psychiatry, 34 Park Street, 3rd Floor Research, New Haven, CT 06508, USA</aff><aff id="I2"><sup>2</sup>Old Age Psychiatry, Institute of Psychiatry, De Crespigny Park, London SE5 8AF, United Kingdom</aff><aff id="I3"><sup>3</sup>Laboratoire de Neurosciences Cognitives, UMR 5106, 4SGDP Centre, Talence, France</aff><aff id="I4"><sup>4</sup>Institute of Psychiatry, De Crespigny Park, London SE5 8AF, United Kingdom</aff><author-notes><corresp id="cor1">*Yann S. Mineur: <email>yann.mineur@yale.edu</email></corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>2005</year></pub-date><volume>12</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>299</fpage><lpage>310</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 2005.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2005</copyright-year></permissions><abstract><p>In the current minireview, we focus on genetic mouse models of Alzheimer's disease (AD). Because various excellent, up-to-date reviews, special issues, and reliable websites are already dedicated to the genetics of Alzheimer's disease in general and of animal models in particular, this review is not meant to be comprehensive. Rather, we aim to steer the Alzheimer's novice through the recent mouse literature on AD. Special attention will be paid to genetic models that have been tested behaviorally.</p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>